{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791576882,"nonce":"mcEr9wE85zYGKMgK/Z+yS5bQEk4=","title":"Compiler: the HIR's id-keyed maps hash with SipHash","message":"HirFunction's blocks, values and locals and HirModule's functions, globals, types, effects and handlers are IndexMaps with the default RandomState, so every lookup by HirId (a u32) runs SipHash-1-3: seeded, built to resist chosen keys, and the same full rounds for a 4-byte key as for a long one. Every pass, the interpreter's bytecode compile and both backends look blocks and values up by id.\n\nOn the Mac at 2ff70e62fb47ac2bad42f2e4a957f34cc10b7c4b, a Lua driver that load()s benchmarks/nbody.lua 400 times (each load lowers, optimizes and compiles its chunk), sampled at 1 ms: SipHash, RandomState and hash-table frames are 13.8% of the busy samples (1109 of 8058), with IndexMap\u003cHirId, HirBlock/HirValue\u003e lookups and inserts among the leaves.\n\nIds are integers from one process-wide counter; a multiplicative hash (Fx) spreads them for one multiply. Dense per-function numbering would avoid hashing altogether but is a redesign of the id model (snapshots relocate ids; OSR and hot reload match on them).","files":null}]}